These appear in the employer’s “What We Look For” section and are treated as eligibility requirements. The posting does not create a separate “Preferred” qualifications section.
Methods, tools, and technical topics named in the role description

Prior-experience status: The posting does not explicitly label these tools or methods “required” or “preferred.” They are described as tools the intern will use and methods the intern will learn or encounter; do not interpret every item as a prerequisite.
•	Tools named: Python, SQL, Snowflake data lake, Copilot Studio, open-source large language models (LLMs), Vision Language Models, decision-intelligence tools, Azure Data Factory, Matillion, Power BI, and advanced analytics tools.
•	Data-science methods: Data preparation, exploratory analysis, feature engineering, and model development.
•	AI/ML topics: Machine learning and AI; forecasting, classification, anomaly detection, and emerging Generative AI applications. The posting phrases these as exposure/learning goals.

Additional expectations implied by duties (not separately labeled as formal qualifications)
•	Partner with experienced teammates and translate business challenges into analytical questions.
•	Participate in discussions, brainstorming, and knowledge-sharing with technical and business teams.
•	Present findings, insights, and recommendations; accept feedback and develop communication skills.
•	Use mentorship and professional-development discussions, remain open to continuous learning, and understand the project lifecycle from problem definition to implementation and measurement.
Close-call rationale. It describes several technologies as tools used on the job, rather than prerequisites. In the diagram, Python and SQL therefore overlap as named skills/tools, while their required level is not assumed to be identical across the two positions.
 
